<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demetrius I of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">337–283 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Demetrius Poliorcetes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demetrius I, called Poliorcetes, 'the Besieger', was a Macedonian general, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> I. He freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 307 BCE and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 305–304 BCE with huge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a>, without success. He seized the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in 294 BCE, displacing the Antipatrids, but lost it in 288 BCE and died a captive of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Demetrius I of Macedon" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Demetrios_Poliorketes_MAN_Napoli_Inv6149.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
